2026-03-17 16:01:35 - DEBUG - all.stratus.diagnosis - [Loop 5] Tool call - base_agent.py:llm_tool_call_step:102 [Full Prompt = [SystemMessage(content="Monitor and diagnose an application consisting of **MANY** microservices. Some or none of the microservices have faults. Get all the pods and deployments to figure out what kind of services are running in the cluster.  Carefully identify the whether the faults are present and if they are, and identify what is the root cause of the fault.\nStop diagnosis once you've found the root cause of the faults.\nGo as deep as you can into what is causing the issue.\nYour instructions to the tools must be clear and concise. Your queries to tools need to be single turn.\nRemember to check these, and remember this information: ## Workloads (Applications) - **Pod**: The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes, representing a single instance of a running application. Can contain one or more tightly coupled containers. - **ReplicaSet**: Ensures that a specified number of pod replicas are running at all times. Often managed indirectly through Deployments. - **Deployment**: Manages the deployment and lifecycle of applications. Provides declarative updates for Pods and ReplicaSets. - **StatefulSet**: Manages stateful applications with unique pod identities and stable storage. Used for workloads like databases. - **DaemonSet**: Ensures that a copy of a specific pod runs on every node in the cluster. Useful for node monitoring agents, log collectors, etc. - **Job**: Manages batch processing tasks that are expected to complete successfully. Ensures pods run to completion. - **CronJob**: Schedules jobs to run at specified times or intervals (similar to cron in Linux).\n## Networking - **Service**: Provides a stable network endpoint for accessing a group of pods. Types: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, and ExternalName. - **Ingress**: Manages external HTTP(S) access to services in the cluster. Supports routing and load balancing for HTTP(S) traffic. - **NetworkPolicy**: Defines rules for network communication between pods and other entities. Used for security and traffic control.\n## Storage - **PersistentVolume (PV)**: Represents a piece of storage in the cluster, provisioned by an administrator or dynamically. - **PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)**: Represents a request for storage by a user. Binds to a PersistentVolume. - **StorageClass**: Defines different storage tiers or backends for dynamic provisioning of PersistentVolumes. - **ConfigMap**: Stores configuration data as key-value pairs for applications. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive data like passwords, tokens, or keys in an encrypted format.\n## Configuration and Metadata - **Namespace**: Logical partitioning of resources within the cluster for isolation and organization. - **ConfigMap**: Provides non-sensitive configuration data in key-value format. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive configuration data securely. - **ResourceQuota**: Restricts resource usage (e.g., CPU, memory) within a namespace. - **LimitRange**: Enforces minimum and maximum resource limits for containers in a namespace.\n## Cluster Management - **Node**: Represents a worker machine in the cluster (virtual or physical). Runs pods and is managed by the control plane. - **ClusterRole and Role**: Define permissions for resources at the cluster or namespace level. - **ClusterRoleBinding and RoleBinding**: Bind roles to users or groups for authorization. - **ServiceAccount**: Associates processes in pods with permissions for accessing the Kubernetes API.\nAfter you finished, submit the detected faulty components to the orchestrator for evaluation. The submission should be a natural language description of the root cause of the failure.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='eeabd0f8-2f54-462e-9fe3-80b19a63932b'), HumanMessage(content="You will be working this application:\nOpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop\nHere are some descriptions about the application:\nAn online shopping platform built with a microservices architecture, showcasing OpenTelemetry instrumentation for distributed tracing across services.\nIt belongs to this namespace:\nastronomy-shop\nIn each round, there is a thinking stage. In the thinking stage, you are given a list of tools. Think about what you want to call. Return your tool choice and the reasoning behind When choosing the tool, refer to the tool by its name. Then, there is a tool-call stage, where you make a tool_call consistent with your explanation. You can run up to 20 rounds to finish the tasks. If you call submit_tool in tool-call stage, the process will end immediately. If you exceed this limitation, the system will force you to make a submission. You will begin by analyzing the service's state and telemetry with the tools.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='5439ab82-295d-4b8c-bb1c-f88484dc9346'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Here are all the tools you can use:\ntool name: get_traces\n\ntool description: Get Jaeger traces for a given service in the last n minutes.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service for which to retrieve trace data.\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n\n\ntool name: get_services\n\ntool description: Retrieve the list of service names from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of service names available in Grafana.\n\n\ntool name: get_operations\n\ntool description: Query available operations for a specific service from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service whose operations should be retrieved.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of operation names associated with the specified service.\n\n\ntool name: get_dependency_graph\n\ntool description: Get service dependency graph from Jaeger\'s native dependencies API.\n    Args:\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n    Returns:\n        str: JSON object representing the dependency graph.\n\n\ntool name: get_metrics\n\ntool description: Query real-time metrics data from the Prometheus instance.\n\n    Args:\n        query (str): A Prometheus Query Language (PromQL) expression used to fetch metric values.\n\n    Returns:\n        dict: The raw Prometheus response containing metric results, including timestamps, values, and labels.\n\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\n\ntool description: This is a tool used to execute read-only kubectl commands.\n\n    Args:\n        command (str): The read-only kubectl command you want to execute in a CLI to manage a k8s cluster. It should start with "kubectl".\n        Available Read-only Commands:\n        "kubectl api-resources",\n        "kubectl api-version",\n        "kubectl auth can-i",\n        "kubectl cluster-info",\n        "kubectl describe",\n        "kubectl diff",\n        "kubectl events",\n        "kubectl explain",\n        "kubectl get",\n        "kubectl logs",\n        "kubectl options",\n        "kubectl top",\n        "kubectl version",\n        "kubectl config view",\n        "kubectl config current-context",\n        "kubectl config get",\n        INTERACTIVE COMMANDS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g. "kubectl edit"\n        INTERACTIVE FLAGS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g., "kubectl logs -f"\n\n\ntool name: submit_tool\n\ntool description: The tool to submit benchmark results\n\n    Args:\n        ans (str): the answer you would like to submit to the benchmark. Use natural language to describe the root cause of the failure.\n\n\nChoose a tool from the list and output the tool name. Justify your tool choice. In the next step, you will generate a tool call for this tool', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='1002311d-c637-4db1-8e5d-73eea29f7a73'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's start by understanding the problem. The user wants me to monitor and diagnose an application called OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop, which is a microservices-based platform. The goal is to identify any faults and determine their root causes. \n\nFirst, I need to figure out what services are running in the cluster. The application is in the namespace 'astronomy-shop'. The tools available include 'get_services' which retrieves service names from Grafana. That seems like a good starting point because knowing the services will help me check each one for issues.\n\nSo, I'll use 'get_services' to list all the services in the Grafana instance. Once I have the list, I can then check each service's metrics, traces, and dependencies to see if there are any anomalies. For example, if a service is down or has high error rates, that could indicate a problem. \n\nAfter getting the services, maybe I'll use 'get_metrics' to look at real-time metrics like error rates or latency. If there are spikes in errors or high latency, that could point to faulty services. Then, using 'get_traces' for specific services to see if there are any failed transactions or long-running requests. \n\nAlso, the 'get_dependency_graph' could show if there are any broken dependencies between services. If a service isn't communicating properly with others, that might be a root cause. \n\nBut first, I need the list of services. So the first step is to call 'get_services' to get all the service names in the 'astronomy-shop' namespace. That way, I can systematically check each one.\n</think>\n\nget_services  \nTo begin diagnosing the application, I need to identify all the services running in the 'astronomy-shop' namespace. The `get_services` tool will retrieve the list of service names from Grafana, providing a foundation for further analysis of their health, metrics, and dependencies. This is the first step in understanding the application's architecture and identifying potential fault points.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 411, 'prompt_tokens': 1492, 'total_tokens': 1903, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc86-a3ce-7580-8df6-c320bf5c7eab-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1492, 'output_tokens': 411, 'total_tokens': 1903}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='9e4ec846-0cf6-4698-a932-e816bb85c1f7'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user wants me to monitor and diagnose an application with many microservices. The application is called OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop, and it\'s in the namespace astronomy-shop. The goal is to figure out if there are any faults and identify the root cause.\n\nIn the previous step, I chose the get_services tool to retrieve the list of service names from Grafana. Now, I need to generate a tool call for that. The get_services tool doesn\'t require any arguments, so the command should be straightforward. The response will give me the list of services, which is essential to know which services are part of the application. Once I have the service names, I can proceed to check each one\'s health, metrics, and dependencies. \n\nWait, but the user mentioned that some or none of the microservices might have faults. So, after getting the services, the next step would be to check their metrics, maybe look into logs, or check their dependencies. But first, I need the list of services. The get_services tool is the right choice here. Let me make sure I\'m using the correct tool and parameters. The tool doesn\'t need any parameters, so the call should be just invoking get_services. \n\nI should also remember that the user is in the thinking stage, and the next step is to generate the tool call. So the correct JSON object would be {"name": "get_services", "arguments": {}}. That\'s all. No arguments needed. Once I get the services, I can move on to other tools like get_metrics or get_dependency_graph. But for now, the first step is to list all services in the namespace. \n\nWait, but the get_services tool\'s description says it retrieves service names from Grafana. Does that mean it\'s specific to the astronomy-shop namespace? The user mentioned the namespace, but the tool\'s description doesn\'t specify. Maybe I should check if there\'s a way to filter by namespace, but according to the tool\'s parameters, there\'s no namespace argument. Hmm. Maybe the Grafana instance is already scoped to the namespace, or perhaps the tool returns all services in the cluster. But the user specified the namespace, so maybe I need to use kubectl to get services in that namespace. Wait, but the available tools include exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd. \n\nWait, the user\'s initial instructions say to use the provided tools. The get_services tool is part of the available tools. But if the get_services tool doesn\'t take a namespace parameter, maybe it\'s not filtering correctly. However, the user mentioned the namespace is astronomy-shop, so perhaps the Grafana instance is already set to that namespace, or the tool\'s response includes services from that namespace. \n\nBut since the tool\'s parameters don\'t include a namespace, I have to assume that the get_services tool returns all services in the cluster. However, the user is focusing on the astronomy-shop namespace. Maybe I should first use kubectl to get the services in that namespace. But the available tools include exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd. So perhaps I should use that to run \'kubectl get services -n astronomy-shop\' to get the list. \n\nWait, but the user\'s initial step was to use get_services. Maybe the get_services tool is designed to get services from Grafana, which might be a different source. But the user\'s application is in a specific namespace. This is a bit confusing. However, the user\'s first step was to use get_services, so maybe I should proceed with that. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the get_services tool is part of the monitoring stack and returns services that are being monitored. But without more info, I have to follow the user\'s initial choice. So the correct tool call is get_services with no arguments. \n\nSo the tool call would be:\n\n{"name": "get_services", "arguments": {}}\n\nThat\'s the correct step here. Once I have the list of services, I can then check each one\'s metrics, dependencies, and logs. But for now, the first step is to get the services.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{}', 'name': 'get_services'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-baab4e73389b1e32', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 852, 'prompt_tokens': 2473, 'total_tokens': 3325, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc86-b776-7ab1-921e-b043fca4d62d-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_services', 'args': {}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-baab4e73389b1e32', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2473, 'output_tokens': 852, 'total_tokens': 3325}), ToolMessage(content='meta=None content=[TextContent(type=\'text\', text="[\'quote\', \'image-provider\', \'load-generator\', \'checkout\', \'cart\', \'frontend\', \'flagd\', \'fraud-detection\', \'email\', \'payment\', \'frontend-proxy\', \'recommendation\', \'product-catalog\', \'currency\', \'shipping\', \'product-reviews\', \'accounting\']", annotations=None)] isError=False', id='b91a4cd3-b0f9-4b22-9b63-01e6066a5a79', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-baab4e73389b1e32'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='302fdaa7-1abe-4323-9ea1-d7f9dbbbb93d'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, so the user is trying to diagnose an issue in the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop, which is a microservices application. They\'ve already used the get_services tool to list all the services in the namespace. The next step is to figure out which of these services might be faulty.\n\nFirst, I need to recall the tools available. There\'s get_traces, get_dependency_graph, get_metrics, and others. Since the user mentioned that some or none of the microservices have faults, I should probably start by checking the health of each service. But how?\n\nThe get_metrics tool can query Prometheus for real-time metrics. Maybe I can check for errors or latency issues. Alternatively, the get_traces tool could help identify issues in the distributed tracing. But without knowing which service is problematic, it\'s hard to narrow down. \n\nWait, the get_dependency_graph might show dependencies between services. If there\'s a service that\'s not responding, the dependency graph could highlight that. But the user might need to check each service\'s metrics first. \n\nAlternatively, using get_operations on each service to see what operations are available. But that might not directly indicate faults. \n\nAnother approach: check the pods and deployments to see if any are in a bad state. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd can be used with "kubectl get pods" and "kubectl get deployments" in the astronomy-shop namespace. That would show the current state of the workloads. If any pods are crashing or deployments are not stable, that\'s a starting point. \n\nBut the user hasn\'t checked the actual pod statuses yet. So maybe the next step is to use kubectl to get the pods and deployments. Let me check the available commands. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd can run "kubectl get pods" and "kubectl get deployments" in the specified namespace. \n\nSo, first, get the list of pods to see if any are in CrashLoopBackOff, Error, or similar states. Then check deployments to see their replicas and availability. That would give a clear picture of the current workload status. \n\nTherefore, the next tool should be exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop" to check pod statuses. Then maybe "kubectl get deployments -n astronomy-shop" to check deployments. But since each tool call is a single command, I should start with the pods first.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nTo diagnose potential faults, I need to check the current state of pods and deployments in the `astronomy-shop` namespace. Using `kubectl get pods` will reveal if any pods are in error states (e.g., CrashLoopBackOff, Error), while `kubectl get deployments` will show deployment statuses and replica availability. This will help identify workload-level issues before diving into metrics or traces. \n\n**Next step**: Run `kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop` to check pod statuses.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 596, 'prompt_tokens': 1725, 'total_tokens': 2321, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc86-e05d-7a32-8301-f054c4fae766-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1725, 'output_tokens': 596, 'total_tokens': 2321}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='2b7ef3f7-5ced-4ea6-a66b-f78a5c892d69'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, the user wants me to generate a tool call based on the previous choice of using exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd. The justification was to check pod and deployment statuses in the astronomy-shop namespace. The next step is to run kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop. So, the command should be "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop". Let me make sure the command is correctly formatted and includes the namespace. Yes, that\'s right. The tool call needs to have the command parameter starting with "kubectl" and include the namespace flag. Alright, that should retrieve the pod statuses.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bf9046bac5524ac8', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 153, 'prompt_tokens': 2736, 'total_tokens': 2889, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc86-fb6d-7a53-acd3-1ef5e926016a-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bf9046bac5524ac8', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2736, 'output_tokens': 153, 'total_tokens': 2889}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                               READY   STATUS    RESTARTS       AGE\naccounting-844d99ddff-hgqdz        1/1     Running   0              3m15s\nad-6f9964ffd8-fgkpl                1/1     Running   0              3m15s\ncart-65474cd577-6xnnr              1/1     Running   0              3m15s\ncheckout-648b497649-wsltl          1/1     Running   0              3m14s\ncurrency-69f5b55bbc-zh4f2          1/1     Running   0              3m14s\nemail-56985bb4f8-v4sv9             1/1     Running   0              3m13s\nflagd-79c4c6cb44-pndvw             2/2     Running   0              3m12s\nfraud-detection-88b76487c-2jbxp    1/1     Running   0              3m12s\nfrontend-65c7cd5577-85vtt          1/1     Running   0              3m10s\nfrontend-proxy-84788b44b-4rhll     1/1     Running   0              3m9s\ngrafana-bdbfb995f-j96pb            4/4     Running   0              3m9s\nimage-provider-6c7d4665cc-6z789    1/1     Running   0              3m7s\njaeger-6d6f47c768-vm7rc            1/1     Running   0              3m6s\nkafka-74ccb7b5fd-vh8gz             1/1     Running   0              3m6s\nllm-55b9d48bb4-82lcp               1/1     Running   0              3m6s\nload-generator-9895cc8bf-sxczd     1/1     Running   0              3m5s\nopensearch-0                       1/1     Running   0              2m43s\notel-collector-agent-6b5mj         1/1     Running   0              3m2s\notel-collector-agent-8vvzl         1/1     Running   0              2m26s\notel-collector-agent-t8mzq         1/1     Running   0              2m58s\npayment-fbfc99665-fbwht            1/1     Running   0              3m\npostgresql-68c5b8f59b-hlflb        1/1     Running   0              2m59s\nproduct-catalog-f57789b6d-6gk69    1/1     Running   2 (2m3s ago)   2m58s\nproduct-reviews-6f447976bc-hrnnc   1/1     Running   0              2m57s\nquote-bc9b88d7d-xmr4p              1/1     Running   0              2m56s\nrecommendation-77b6cd7dc6-bq7xk    1/1     Running   0              2m55s\nshipping-54f9758cb5-2475c          1/1     Running   0              2m54s\nvalkey-cart-6c4f7cf8f6-mgdcs       1/1     Running   0              2m53s\n', id='91c28094-a3bf-4cc7-93f5-b0ec499643f6', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-bf9046bac5524ac8'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='391d5d58-13b9-459d-9f78-a0840e499028'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to monitor and diagnose the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application, which has many microservices. So far, they\'ve used get_services to list all the services and exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to check the pod statuses. All the pods are running with status "Running" and no restarts, so the initial check didn\'t show any obvious issues.\n\nNow, the next step is to check if there are any underlying issues that aren\'t visible from just the pod statuses. Since the problem might be related to dependencies between services, maybe network issues, or resource constraints, I should consider looking into the service dependency graph. The get_dependency_graph tool can show how services are connected. If there\'s a service that\'s not communicating properly with others, that could be a root cause.\n\nAlternatively, checking metrics with get_metrics could help identify if any services are underperforming or consuming too many resources. But since the user mentioned that some or none of the microservices have faults, maybe the issue is in the dependencies or network policies. \n\nAnother angle is to look into specific services that might be critical. For example, if the payment service is down, that would affect the entire shopping process. But since all pods are running, maybe the problem is in the services\' configurations or their interactions. \n\nThe get_dependency_graph tool would provide a visual representation of how services are interconnected. If there\'s a service that\'s not properly connected or has a high error rate in dependencies, that could indicate a problem. So, using get_dependency_graph with a reasonable time frame, like the last 10 minutes, would help identify any anomalies in the service interactions. \n\nAlternatively, using get_traces for a specific service might show if there are any errors in the traces. But without knowing which service is faulty, starting with the dependency graph makes sense to get an overview. \n\nSo, the best tool here is get_dependency_graph with last_n_minutes set to 10 to capture recent dependencies and spot any issues in the service interactions.\n</think>\n\nget_dependency_graph  \nTo identify potential issues in service interactions, I need to examine the dependency graph to detect anomalies like missing connections, excessive latency, or failed communication between microservices. This will reveal if any services are not properly integrated or experiencing dependency-related faults.  \n\n**Next step**: Retrieve the dependency graph for the last 10 minutes to analyze service interactions.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 493, 'prompt_tokens': 2836, 'total_tokens': 3329, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc87-0685-78e0-a80f-450a3b650659-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2836, 'output_tokens': 493, 'total_tokens': 3329}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='e19f001a-925a-488d-af25-27fea68f5a63'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose an application with many microservices. They've already checked the pods and deployments using kubectl and found all pods are running normally. Now they want to look into the dependency graph to see how the services interact.\n\nThe tool get_dependency_graph is the right choice here. It fetches the service dependencies from Jaeger's native dependencies API. By looking at the dependency graph, they can see if there are any services that aren't properly connected or if there's a chain of dependencies causing issues. \n\nThe user needs to specify a time frame, so setting last_n_minutes to 10 makes sense to check recent interactions. This will help identify any recent problems in service communication. The response from the tool will be a JSON object, which they can then analyze for any anomalies or errors in the service dependencies.\n\nSo, the next step is to call get_dependency_graph with last_n_minutes=10. That should provide the necessary data to check for dependency-related faults.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"last_n_minutes": 10}', 'name': 'get_dependency_graph'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-ba1bd2f2b6618546', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 233, 'prompt_tokens': 3814, 'total_tokens': 4047, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc87-1e09-7d30-b211-558c0f7ceae5-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_dependency_graph', 'args': {'last_n_minutes': 10}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-ba1bd2f2b6618546', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3814, 'output_tokens': 233, 'total_tokens': 4047}), ToolMessage(content='meta=None content=[TextContent(type=\'text\', text="{\'data\': [{\'parent\': \'frontend\', \'child\': \'product-reviews\', \'callCount\': 24}, {\'parent\': \'checkout\', \'child\': \'product-catalog\', \'callCount\': 11}, {\'parent\': \'load-generator\', \'child\': \'frontend-proxy\', \'callCount\': 237}, {\'parent\': \'shipping\', \'child\': \'quote\', \'callCount\': 6}, {\'parent\': \'frontend\', \'child\': \'checkout\', \'callCount\': 7}, {\'parent\': \'recommendation\', \'child\': \'product-catalog\', \'callCount\': 14}, {\'parent\': \'frontend\', \'child\': \'cart\', \'callCount\': 60}, {\'parent\': \'product-reviews\', \'child\': \'flagd\', \'callCount\': 2}, {\'parent\': \'frontend\', \'child\': \'product-catalog\', \'callCount\': 144}, {\'parent\': \'checkout\', \'child\': \'email\', \'callCount\': 6}, {\'parent\': \'fraud-detection\', \'child\': \'flagd\', \'callCount\': 1}, {\'parent\': \'cart\', \'child\': \'flagd\', \'callCount\': 9}, {\'parent\': \'frontend-proxy\', \'child\': \'frontend\', \'callCount\': 235}, {\'parent\': \'checkout\', \'child\': \'payment\', \'callCount\': 6}, {\'parent\': \'checkout\', \'child\': \'cart\', \'callCount\': 12}, {\'parent\': \'payment\', \'child\': \'flagd\', \'callCount\': 1}, {\'parent\': \'checkout\', \'child\': \'shipping\', \'callCount\': 12}, {\'parent\': \'recommendation\', \'child\': \'flagd\', \'callCount\': 1}, {\'parent\': \'checkout\', \'child\': \'currency\', \'callCount\': 17}, {\'parent\': \'frontend\', \'child\': \'recommendation\', \'callCount\': 17}], \'total\': 0, \'limit\': 0, \'offset\': 0, \'errors\': None}", annotations=None)] isError=False', id='3006537d-24d0-4d54-959a-1844f75c37d8', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-ba1bd2f2b6618546'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='e03f3786-0610-4d55-9299-bf8cad473e7b'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose faults in the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. So far, they\'ve checked the services using get_services, looked at the pods and deployments with kubectl, and examined the dependency graph. All the pods are running, and the dependency graph shows call counts between services. But maybe there\'s something else going on.\n\nThe next step is to check if there are any errors or issues in the service interactions. The dependency graph shows call counts, but not errors. Maybe they should look into metrics to see if any services are experiencing high latency or failures. The get_metrics tool can query Prometheus for real-time metrics. For example, checking for failed requests or latency outliers. \n\nAlternatively, they could look into traces with get_traces for a specific service to see if there are any errors in the distributed transactions. But since the user hasn\'t found any obvious issues yet, maybe checking metrics first would be better. Let\'s pick a service that\'s part of the dependency graph and check its metrics. For example, the frontend service has many calls to product-catalog and cart. Checking the frontend\'s error rate or latency could be useful. \n\nSo, using get_metrics with a query that looks for errors or high latency in the frontend service. The query could be something like "sum(rate(http_requests_total{job=\'frontend\'}[5m]))" to get the request count, or "http_request_duration_seconds_count{job=\'frontend\'}" for latency. Wait, but the exact metric names might vary. Maybe they should use a more specific query. Alternatively, check for failed requests: "sum(rate(http_requests_total{job=\'frontend\', status_code!~\\"2[0-9][0-9]\\"}[5m]))". \n\nBut since the user is using Prometheus, they need to know the exact metric names. If they\'re not sure, maybe start with a general query. Alternatively, check for any anomalies in the dependency graph. Since the dependency graph doesn\'t show errors, maybe the issue is in the metrics. So, the next tool should be get_metrics to investigate further.\n</think>\n\nget_metrics  \nTo investigate potential performance issues or errors in service interactions, I need to query real-time metrics (e.g., error rates, latency, or failed requests) using Prometheus. This will help identify if any services are experiencing anomalies like high latency, failed requests, or resource exhaustion.  \n\n**Next step**: Check for failed HTTP requests in the `frontend` service, which has high interaction volume. Query: `sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="frontend", status_code!~"2[0-9][0-9]"}[5m]))`.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 554, 'prompt_tokens': 3454, 'total_tokens': 4008, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc87-2a82-7310-8360-1e60173a5828-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3454, 'output_tokens': 554, 'total_tokens': 4008}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='35984ca5-1f30-46d2-88bd-ba6835bcc143'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. They\'ve already checked the pods and deployments, and all seem to be running normally. Then they looked at the dependency graph, which shows the services and their interactions. Now, they want to check metrics to find any anomalies.\n\nThe next step was to use the get_metrics tool with a specific PromQL query to check for failed HTTP requests in the frontend service. The query they mentioned is `sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="frontend", status_code!~"2[0-9][0-9]"}[5m]))`. This query calculates the rate of HTTP requests that didn\'t result in a 2xx status code over the last 5 minutes, which indicates errors.\n\nI need to make sure that the tool call is correctly formatted. The function name is get_metrics, and the argument is the query. The user\'s previous steps have led them to this point, so executing this query will provide the necessary data to identify if there are any failed requests. If the result shows a high number of errors, that would point to a problem with the frontend service or its dependencies. If not, they might need to look into other metrics or traces. \n\nSince the user is following a logical flow from checking pod statuses, dependency graphs, to metrics, this step is crucial for pinpointing specific issues. The answer should then be based on the metrics data retrieved. If there are errors, they can proceed to check traces or logs. If not, maybe the issue is elsewhere, like in the database or external services. But since the user wants the root cause, this metric check is a key part of the diagnosis.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"query": "sum(rate(http_requests_total{job=\\"frontend\\", status_code!~\\"2[0-9][0-9]\\"}[5m]))"}', 'name': 'get_metrics'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-aede5c2aea86c50c', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 403, 'prompt_tokens': 4474, 'total_tokens': 4877, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc87-444f-71f3-8752-7da194e215b4-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_metrics', 'args': {'query': 'sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="frontend", status_code!~"2[0-9][0-9]"}[5m]))'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-aede5c2aea86c50c', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4474, 'output_tokens': 403, 'total_tokens': 4877}), ToolMessage(content='("{\'resultType\': \'vector\', \'result\': []}", 12)', id='6dead2e9-3b55-4435-8753-357da4ae7023', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-aede5c2aea86c50c'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='46701b92-0097-40a1-b4f6-09deca9eb2b2'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. So far, they've checked the services list, pod statuses, and the dependency graph. They also tried to get metrics for the frontend service's failed HTTP requests but got an empty result. \n\nHmm, the next step. Since the frontend's error metric query returned nothing, maybe there are no failed requests there. But the user needs to check other services too. Maybe look at other services with high call counts from the dependency graph. For example, the load-generator has a high call count to frontend-proxy. Maybe check if frontend-proxy is having issues.\n\nAlternatively, check the metrics for other services like checkout, which has several dependencies. Or maybe look at the payment service, which is called by checkout. Also, the product-catalog is called by multiple services. Maybe check if there are any errors in those services. \n\nAnother approach: use the get_traces tool to look into specific services. If there are no errors in metrics, maybe the traces would show latency issues or failed transactions. For example, checking the checkout service's traces could reveal if there are any failed transactions or long-running requests. \n\nWait, the user might need to check the traces for services that have high interaction volumes. The dependency graph shows that frontend has a lot of calls to cart and product-catalog. Maybe checking the cart service's traces could show if there are any issues. \n\nAlternatively, check the payment service, which is called by checkout. If payment is having issues, that could cause problems in the checkout process. \n\nSo, the next tool call should be get_traces for a service with high activity. Let's pick the checkout service since it's called by multiple services and has a few call counts. Using get_traces with a reasonable time frame, like the last 10 minutes, to see if there are any errors or anomalies in the traces.\n</think>\n\nget_traces  \nTo investigate potential issues in service interactions, I will examine traces for the **checkout** service, which has multiple dependencies (e.g., `cart`, `payment`, `shipping`). This will help identify errors, latency spikes, or failed transactions in critical paths.  \n\n**Next step**: Retrieve traces for the `checkout` service over the last 10 minutes.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, 